# A7 6.06  Real-World Coordinate-Plane Problems; Perimeter — MA.8.GR.1.2 (MN 6.6). A perimeter on the grid adds every
# side, and a slanted side is the hypotenuse of its own right triangle; a triangle with no right angle is split by its
# height into two (the book's move). On a map the streets add the legs and the straight line is the hypotenuse. An
# irrational total is kept exact and rounded once, at the end. No distance formula (Clarification 2); every radicand a
# whole number up to 225 with no square factor, so the whole slanted sides are 5, 10, 13 and 15 only: 3-4-5 and 6-8-10
# carry the warm-up, the notes and the examples, 5-12-13 and 9-12-15 the practice (ruling 43).
# Built from the benchmark and the B1G-M first (the unit outline), then read against Math Nation 6.6 (A7 Unit 6 Audit).
